    "result_text": "Done. The full build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 3/3 tests passing).\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization applied at owner creation, keeping the change small and consistent with the codebase's existing patterns.\n\n### Changes\n\n**1. New `AddressNormalizer` util** (`util/AddressNormalizer.java`)\nA single-responsibility utility mirroring the existing `TextNormalizer`/`TelephoneNormalizer`/`EmailNormalizer`. It trims, collapses internal whitespace to single spaces, upper-cases, and expands common street-type abbreviations per word (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). Returns `null` for `null` input.\n\n**2. Wired it into `OwnerMapper.toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)`**\nAdded one `@Mapping` expression for `address`, right alongside the identical existing normalizations for `telephone` and `email`. This is the DTO\u2192entity boundary hit on every create, so the **stored and returned** address is the normalized form.\n\n### Why this satisfies the spec\n\n- **Store & return normalized** \u2014 the mapper sets the canonical value before `createOwner` persists it, and the response DTO is built from that same owner.\n- **Required-field check rejects blank-after-normalization** \u2014 the address's `pattern: '.*\\S.*'` (openapi DTO validation) rejects all-whitespace input with 400. Since normalization only strips whitespace, \"blank after normalization\" is exactly the all-whitespace raw case that check already covers \u2014 so the reject case (`\"   \"` \u2192 400) is enforced correctly.\n- **Household duplicate detection & shared household id use the normalized form** \u2014 `findHouseholdMembers` and `HouseholdIdGenerator` both read `owner.getAddress()`, which now holds the normalized value. This delivers the behaviour change: `\"12 main st\"` and `\"12 MAIN STREET\"` now both canonicalize to `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`, so they're detected as the same household.\n\nI deliberately did **not** touch `updateOwner` (the spec scopes this to owner *creation*) and did not reuse `TextNormalizer` for the stored value, since that lowercases and does no expansion \u2014 it remains the comparison-key primitive it was designed to be.",
